From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 19:07:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 81/83] fix(witness): rotate the reaper's cutoff and evidence
 the promotion criterion (gcp-schs)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The `--no-dry-run` promotion gate for polecat-worktree-reap.sh was evaluated on a
population that structurally excluded part of the candidate set, in a biased way.
The reaper enumerates deterministically and `break`s out of that ordered loop when
its wall-clock budget expires, so the candidates it dropped were ALWAYS the same
sorted tail — never a rotating or random slice. winnow's two 2026-09-06 cycles
examined the identical prefix (2 of 12, then 8 of 11) and the tail appeared in
neither; 50 budget_exhausted events city-wide say this is the normal case at load,
not an outlier.

That is not a fairness nicety. The criterion for flipping the staged rollout live
is a REVIEWED would-reap set, and it silently assumed reviewed-set == candidate-set.
Under a fixed cutoff the criterion can be satisfied in full — N cycles, no live
worktree ever seen — while the tail has never been examined once, and the tail is
exactly what a live reaper running with a fresh budget reaches first. Nothing is at
risk today because the reaper stays in dry-run, but the evidence the operator's flip
decision rests on was corrupt. This is distinct from gcp-uzq0 and gcp-ga3m, which
are both about the budget being too small: fixing either would reduce how often this
bites without making the criterion sound, since one slow cycle reintroduces it.

The bead offered two invariants — rotate the enumeration, or require a `deferred=0`
cycle before the flip is evidenced. This takes both halves, because each is weak
alone: rotation makes coverage eventually complete but gives an operator nothing to
check, and a `deferred=0` requirement is unreachable on a busy rig if every cycle
re-walks the same prefix. So the candidate list is sorted explicitly (order becomes
the script's, not git's), a truncated cycle persists the last candidate it DECIDED
to a per-rig cursor beside the log, and the next cycle resumes after it and wraps —
making the deferred remainder a moving window. A cycle that examines everything now
says so once, as `worktree_scan_complete` with `deferred=0`, and clears the cursor
so the next cycle starts at the head rather than being pinned mid-list. A candidate
truncated before it was ever inspected holds the cursor back rather than advancing
it, so it stays inside the next cycle's window.

mol-witness-patrol's `reap-merged-worktrees` step now states the criterion on
coverage instead of on a count of clean cycles, with the two jq reads an operator
runs to check it, and the event table carries the new line plus a note that
`worktree_budget_exhausted` is honest but is not evidence for the flip. The witness
prompt gained the same caveat in short form.

Nothing changes about what the reaper removes: the gates, the dry-run default, and
the honest `deferred=N` reporting on a budget-limited cycle are all untouched — only
which candidates a short cycle looks at first, and what the log lets a reader
conclude. Every failure in the new path degrades to "no rotation": an unreadable or
unwritable cursor costs fairness, never the witness start.

Validation: new red/green test drives six truncated cycles over six candidates and
asserts the reviewed set reaches all of them. With rotation disabled it reports the
same three-candidate prefix six cycles running — the defect, reproduced — and passes
with it enabled. It also holds that a truncated cycle never claims a complete scan,
still reports its deferred count, and leaves a cursor, while a complete cycle emits
`worktree_scan_complete` with `deferred=0` and clears it. Full gastown suite (11
scripts) green, shellcheck clean, go vet / golangci-lint / validate_registry /
go test ./... green.

Follow-up: polecat-home-audit.sh has the identical loop shape and the identical
promotion criterion, so it carries the same bias; filed separately rather than
widened into this bead's scope.

